@startuml
' Step-up authentication sequence diagram.
' Render: plantuml -tpng -nometadata -o .. step-up-auth-sequences.puml
skin rose
skinparam dpi 100
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam maxMessageSize 330
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left

title Step-up Authentication Flow (challenge-bound re-authentication)\nMFA required, authenticator app only

actor User
participant UserAgent #LightGreen
participant "Authenticator\napp" as Mobile #Orange
participant "Cognito\n(MFA required)" as Cognito #Orange
participant "Pre token generation\nLambda" as PreToken #Orange
box "Privilege API" #DDDDDD
participant "API Gateway" as PrivApi #LightSalmon
participant "Lambda\nAuthorizer" as Authorizer
participant "Business Logic\nLambda" as Backend
end box
participant "DynamoDB\nAPI Setting" as Setting #LightBlue
box "Step Up Auth APIs" #DDDDDD
participant "API Gateway" as StepUpApi #LightSalmon
participant "Initiate Auth\nLambda" as Initiate
participant "Respond To Challenge\nLambda" as Respond
end box
participant "DynamoDB\nSession" as Session #LightBlue

== First sign-in: set up the authenticator app (MFA_SETUP) ==

User -> UserAgent : first login
UserAgent -> Cognito : InitiateAuth (username, password via SRP)
Cognito --> UserAgent : MFA_SETUP challenge and sign-in session (no tokens yet)
UserAgent -> Cognito : AssociateSoftwareToken (sign-in session)
Cognito --> UserAgent : authenticator secret
UserAgent --> User : show QR code and setup key
User -> Mobile : scan QR code, or enter setup key
Mobile --> User : first code
User -> UserAgent : enter code
UserAgent -> Cognito : VerifySoftwareToken (sign-in session, code)
Cognito --> UserAgent : SUCCESS
UserAgent -> Cognito : RespondToAuthChallenge (MFA_SETUP)
Cognito -> PreToken : pre token generation trigger (V2_0)
PreToken --> Cognito : suppress scope aws.cognito.signin.user.admin
Cognito --> UserAgent : ID token, access token (no admin scope)
note right of Cognito
  MFA can't be turned off afterwards, and no token can
  register or replace the authenticator.  Later sign-ins
  ask for the password, then the authenticator code,
  as in the re-authentication below
end note

== Call a privileged API ==

User -> UserAgent : perform privilege operation\n/transfer
UserAgent -> PrivApi : /transfer\nAuthorization: Bearer <access token>
PrivApi -> Authorizer : Authorize
Authorizer -> Authorizer : verify access token
note right of Authorizer
  no Authorization header: 401
  invalid access token: deny (403)
end note
Authorizer -> Setting : look-up API setting (id = API path)
Setting --> Authorizer : stepUpState, or no setting

alt STEP_UP_DENY, or setting can't be read
  Authorizer --> PrivApi : deny policy
  PrivApi --> UserAgent : 403
else STEP_UP_NOT_REQUIRED, or no setting
  Authorizer --> PrivApi : allow policy (session table not used)
  PrivApi -> Backend : invoke
  Backend --> PrivApi : success
  PrivApi --> UserAgent : 200
else STEP_UP_REQUIRED
  Authorizer -> Session : get session (sessionId = access token jti)
  Session --> Authorizer : session, or not found
  alt stepUpStatus = STEP_UP_COMPLETED and ttl > now
    Authorizer --> PrivApi : allow policy
    PrivApi -> Backend : invoke
    Backend --> PrivApi : success
    PrivApi --> UserAgent : 200
  else no session, ttl passed or missing, or any other stepUpStatus
    Authorizer -> Session : create session (sessionId = access token jti)\nstepUpStatus = STEP_UP_REQUIRED\nttl = min(now + SESSION_TABLE_ITEM_TTL, token exp)
    Session --> Authorizer : session created
    Authorizer --> PrivApi : 401 (Unauthorized)
    PrivApi --> UserAgent : 401
  else session can't be read
    Authorizer --> PrivApi : deny policy
    PrivApi --> UserAgent : 403
  end
end

== Initiate step-up: mint a one-time challenge ==

UserAgent -> StepUpApi : /initiate-auth - triggered by the 401\nIdentification: <ID token>\nAuthorization: Bearer <access token>
StepUpApi -> StepUpApi : Cognito authorizer validates the Identification\nID token; missing or invalid: 401 (before Lambda runs)
StepUpApi -> Initiate : invoke Lambda Proxy
Initiate -> Initiate : verify the access token and require its sub to\nequal the gateway-verified ID token sub\n(invalid or mismatch: 401)
Initiate -> Session : create one-time challenge\nsessionId = challenge#<random id>, userId = sub\ncreateTimestamp = now\nttl = now + STEP_UP_CHALLENGE_TTL
Session --> Initiate : challenge created
Initiate --> StepUpApi : 200 { code: REAUTH_REQUIRED, challengeId }
StepUpApi --> UserAgent : 200 { code: REAUTH_REQUIRED, challengeId }

== Re-authenticate with Cognito ==

UserAgent -> Cognito : sign out, then InitiateAuth\n(user re-enters password)
Cognito --> UserAgent : authenticator code challenge (SOFTWARE_TOKEN_MFA)
User -> Mobile : read current code
Mobile --> User : code
User -> UserAgent : enter code
UserAgent -> Cognito : RespondToAuthChallenge (authenticator code)
Cognito -> PreToken : pre token generation trigger (V2_0)
PreToken --> Cognito : suppress scope aws.cognito.signin.user.admin
Cognito --> UserAgent : FRESH ID token and access token\n(new auth_time, no admin scope)

== Respond to challenge: validate and consume ==

UserAgent -> StepUpApi : /respond-to-challenge\nIdentification: <FRESH ID token>\nAuthorization: Bearer <FRESH access token>\nbody: { challengeId }
StepUpApi -> StepUpApi : Cognito authorizer validates the Identification\nID token; missing or invalid: 401 (before Lambda runs)
StepUpApi -> Respond : invoke Lambda Proxy
note right of Respond
  Identification already passed the gateway authorizer above.
  Here the handler rejects a missing or malformed Authorization
  bearer token with 401 { code: UNAUTHENTICATED } (no reason)
  before any validation runs
end note
Respond -> Respond : verify both tokens; require the same sub
Respond -> Session : get challenge (sessionId = challenge#<challengeId>)
Session --> Respond : challenge, or not found
Respond -> Respond : challenge exists, unexpired and minted for this sub;\naccess token auth_time strictly after the challenge creation\ntime and recent (within STEP_UP_MAX_REAUTH_AGE)
Respond -> Cognito : GetUserPoolMfaConfig (USER_POOL_ID)
Cognito --> Respond : pool MFA setting (must be ON, software token only)
Respond -> Cognito : AdminGetUser (USER_POOL_ID, username from the FRESH token)
Cognito --> Respond : user (must be enabled, with the tokens' sub)

alt a validation check fails (NO_CHALLENGE, CHALLENGE_SUB_MISMATCH, STALE_AUTH, TOKEN_MISMATCH, MFA_NOT_ENROLLED, BAD_ACCESS_TOKEN, BAD_ID_TOKEN, BAD_TOKEN, MFA_CHECK_FAILED)
  Respond --> StepUpApi : 401 { reason } (session table unchanged)
  StepUpApi --> UserAgent : 401 { reason }
else all checks pass
  Respond -> Session : delete challenge (one-time, conditional)
  alt challenge consumed
    Session --> Respond : challenge consumed
    Respond -> Session : create session (sessionId = FRESH access token jti)\nstepUpStatus = STEP_UP_COMPLETED\nttl = min(now + SESSION_TABLE_ITEM_TTL, token exp)
    alt completed row written
      Session --> Respond : session created
      Respond --> StepUpApi : 200 (step-up complete)
      StepUpApi --> UserAgent : 200
    else completed row write fails
      Respond --> StepUpApi : 401 { reason: SESSION_WRITE_FAILED }\nchallenge already spent, so restart from /initiate-auth
      StepUpApi --> UserAgent : 401 { reason: SESSION_WRITE_FAILED }
    end
  else consume fails (e.g. a concurrent completion already consumed it)
    Respond --> StepUpApi : 401 { reason: CHALLENGE_CONSUME_FAILED }\nnothing written
    StepUpApi --> UserAgent : 401 { reason: CHALLENGE_CONSUME_FAILED }
  end
end

UserAgent -> PrivApi : retry /transfer with the FRESH access token
note right of PrivApi
  same flow as above: the fresh token's jti now has a
  STEP_UP_COMPLETED session whose ttl is in the future,
  so the authorizer allows the call (200) until the ttl
  passes.  the original access token stays un-elevated.
end note
@enduml
